# SDP_1 — Burger Builder Pattern

## Project Description

This project demonstrates the Builder Design Pattern using a burger as a real-world example.

A burger can contain different components such as a bun, patty, cheese, sauce, and vegetables. The Builder Pattern allows us to construct a burger step by step instead of using a large constructor with many parameters.

The project is implemented in Java using JDK 17.

## Design Pattern

The project uses the Builder Design Pattern.

The Builder Pattern is useful when an object has multiple components, can have different configurations, and should be created step by step.

In this project, the pattern is used to create different types of burgers.

## Project Structure

Burger — Product. It represents the final burger object and contains the bun, patty, cheese, sauce, and vegetables.

BurgerBuilder — Builder interface. It defines the methods required to construct a burger step by step.

ClassicBurgerBuilder — Concrete Builder. It is used to construct a classic burger with a beef patty and traditional ingredients.

VegetarianBurgerBuilder — Concrete Builder. It is used to construct a vegetarian burger with a veggie patty and different ingredients.

BurgerDirector — Director. It defines reusable construction sequences for different burger configurations.

Main — Client. It creates the builders and the director, builds the burgers, and displays the final results.

## Builder Pattern Structure

Client -> BurgerDirector -> BurgerBuilder -> ClassicBurgerBuilder / VegetarianBurgerBuilder -> Burger

## Example

The project creates two different burgers.

Classic Burger:
Bun: Sesame bun
Patty: Beef
Cheese: Cheddar
Sauce: Ketchup
Vegetables: Lettuce and tomato

Vegetarian Burger:
Bun: Whole wheat bun
Patty: Veggie
Cheese: Mozzarella
Sauce: Garlic sauce
Vegetables: Lettuce, tomato and cucumber

## Fluent API

The Builder Pattern uses method chaining, also called a fluent API.

Example:

builder
    .setBun("Sesame bun")
    .setPatty("Beef")
    .setCheese("Cheddar")
    .setSauce("Ketchup")
    .setVegetables("Lettuce and tomato")
    .build();

This makes the construction process easier to read and understand.

## How to Run

Requirements:
- Java JDK 17
- IntelliJ IDEA

Steps:
1. Open the project in IntelliJ IDEA.
2. Make sure JDK 17 is selected.
3. Open Main.java.
4. Run the main() method.
5. The program will display the classic and vegetarian burgers in the console.

## Advantages of Using Builder Pattern

- Makes complex object construction easier to understand.
- Allows step-by-step construction.
- Supports different configurations of the same product.
- Improves readability through method chaining.
- Separates object construction from the final product.

